From: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: flowtable: tear down flows of dead XFRM input states

A flow whose inbound direction an XFRM packet offload state
authenticated keeps that state, with a reference, in xfrm_input of the
inbound tuple, and both of its tuples keep the policy generation that
the packet was checked against. nft_flow_route() gives the tuples of a
flow with a deferred input state the current generation as well.

Nothing tears such a flow down when its input state is deleted. The
software path hands every packet of a tuple with XFRM input metadata to
the slow path before nf_flow_offload_forward(), without looking at the
state, and the route of that tuple leads to the LAN, so
nf_flow_dst_check() does not notice a change of the inbound SA. Only an
outbound SA change makes the xfrm_dst of the other tuple stale. The
software path does check the policy generation of a tuple it looks up,
but a flow that the hardware forwards in both directions sends it no
packet.

Such a flow lives on until it ends, and with it the reference on the
deleted state. A packet offload driver that releases its resources in
xdo_dev_state_free() keeps them for as long: the EN7581 SOE releases the
SA slot of a state, one of 32, there, so a long-lived connection keeps
the slot of every inbound SA that it outlived reserved. Where the
hardware forwards the inbound tuple, the driver drops the entry of a
deleted SA, and the packets that the new SA decrypts reach the CPU until
the flow ends, because the flow is never created again with the new
state and its flow id.

Tear a flow down in the GC when one of its tuples carries a policy
generation other than the current one, or an input state that is no
longer XFRM_STATE_VALID: deleted, expired or in error. A soft expiry
(km.dying) does not count, as the state stays in use until IKE replaces
it. The next packet of the connection creates the flow again with the
current state and generation. The generation changes with every change
of the policy database, not with SA changes, so XFRM flows are torn down
once per policy change, which the software path already does for the
tuples that it sees. A state dies on rekey, expiry or flush, so a flow
is torn down once per inbound rekey, mostly within the same second as
the teardown that the outbound rekey causes through its stale xfrm_dst.

The check runs for every flow on every GC pass and takes no lock. A
flow without XFRM metadata costs a load and a branch per tuple. The
flow holds its input state until the GC frees the flow, so the state can
be read; xfrm_input is read once and km.state, a byte written under
x->lock, with READ_ONCE().

Signed-off-by: hurrhnn <hurrhnn@icmp.kr>
---
diff -urNp a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
@@ -606,6 +606,41 @@ static void nf_flow_table_extend_ct_time
 	nf_ct_put(ct);
 }
 
+/* The software path hands the packets of a tuple with XFRM input metadata
+ * to the slow path without looking at its input state, and the route of the
+ * tuple does not lead into the tunnel, so neither a packet nor
+ * nf_flow_dst_check() notices that the state was deleted. A flow that the
+ * hardware forwards in both directions does not notice a policy change
+ * either.
+ */
+static bool nf_flow_xfrm_stale(const struct flow_offload *flow)
+{
+#if IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_XFRM)
+	const struct flow_offload_tuple *tuple;
+	const struct xfrm_state *x;
+	int dir;
+
+	for (dir = 0; dir < FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_MAX; dir++) {
+		tuple = &flow->tuplehash[dir].tuple;
+		/* Only a tuple with XFRM input metadata has a generation. */
+		if (!tuple->xfrm_policy_genid)
+			continue;
+
+		if (tuple->xfrm_policy_genid !=
+		    xfrm_policy_generation(nf_ct_net(flow->ct)))
+			return true;
+
+		/* The flow holds a reference on its input state until the GC
+		 * frees the flow, so no lock is needed to look at the state.
+		 */
+		x = READ_ONCE(tuple->xfrm_input);
+		if (x && READ_ONCE(x->km.state) != XFRM_STATE_VALID)
+			return true;
+	}
+#endif
+	return false;
+}
+
 static void nf_flow_offload_gc_step(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table,
 				    struct flow_offload *flow, void *data)
 {
@@ -615,6 +650,7 @@ static void nf_flow_offload_gc_step(stru
 	    nf_ct_is_dying(flow->ct) ||
 	    !nf_flow_dst_check(&flow->tuplehash[FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_ORIGINAL].tuple) ||
 	    !nf_flow_dst_check(&flow->tuplehash[FLOW_OFFLOAD_DIR_REPLY].tuple) ||
+	    nf_flow_xfrm_stale(flow) ||
 	    nf_flow_custom_gc(flow_table, flow)) {
 		flow_offload_teardown(flow);
 		teardown = true;
